Abstract
========
> Inner caches on top of Point of Coherence don't have to be coherent when using 
memory models weaker than weak consistency.

> This applies to JVMs since they use a modified form of Release Consistency. 

> Happens-Before is established using the proposal before that lwo. The main idea is to
  mark inner cache lines that were written to while holding the lock. This set will henceforth
  be called the **WRITE SET**


Details
=======

  lock(lock)
  acquire.fence    &lock->writeset
    .....
    .....
    .....
  release.fence    &lock->writeset
  unlock(lock)
  

acquire.fence
-------------
> acquire.fence has been modified to take in the address of the WRITESET which can be
  or arbitrary size. The goal is to have a bit for each cacheline but a set associative
  MASK to reduce its size works too.

> acquire.fence invalidates lines pointed to by the current WRITESET and zeroes WRITESET

> acquire.fence sets a flag on that core such that all subsequent writes will have a 
  TAG bit set in their cacheline

> [OPEN] What happens to pending TAGGED lines?

release.fence
-------------
> release.fence causes all TAGGED cachelines to be flushed to Point of Coherence (POC)

> release.fence also causes a MASK of all such TAGGED cachelines to be stored in 
  the WRITESET


WRITESET
--------
> when a TAGGED cacheline is evicted or written back, the TAG on that cacheline is removed
  and the WRITESET updated immediately

> the WRITESET can be stored in the inner cache during this operation and flushed out by
  release.fence

> Thread migrations will need to do a inner full flush or be able to locate all pending
  WRITESETS (*) and do a sum of flush

  (*) Multiple WRITESETS are possible due to nested locks
  [OPEN] This is not resolved yet

Biased Locking
==============
> If you couple this with Biased Locking then each thread will have only one WRITESET
  and the acquire.fence / release.fence instructions will be used only when ownership
  changes hands.  This is good for reducing overall system coherence traffic.

